1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,380
Shake it or swirl it?

2
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:03,242
The label already answered.

3
00:00:03,242 --> 00:00:04,218
Here is why.

4
00:00:04,218 --> 00:00:10,013
Inside the vial is the amino-chain, a peptide, folded into a compact shape.

5
00:00:10,013 --> 00:00:12,812
When you shake, you create bubbles and foam.

6
00:00:12,812 --> 00:00:16,354
Every bubble is a new surface where air meets water.

7
00:00:16,354 --> 00:00:20,039
At that surface, some amino-chains can partly unfold.

8
00:00:20,039 --> 00:00:24,947
Unfolded chains can stick to each other and form clumps, called aggregates.

9
00:00:24,947 --> 00:00:30,403
Labs even shake samples on purpose, to force aggregation in stability testing.

10
00:00:30,403 --> 00:00:35,677
A gentle swirl mixes the liquid with almost no new air-water surface.

11
00:00:35,677 --> 00:00:40,976
That is why some labels spell it out: swirl the vial gently until fully dissolved.

12
00:00:40,976 --> 00:00:42,903
Gentle is not a suggestion.

13
00:00:42,903 --> 00:00:44,361
It is part of the method.

14
00:00:44,361 --> 00:00:45,716
Day seven of ten.

15
00:00:45,716 --> 00:00:47,630
Follow so you don't miss day eight.

